The '''1978–79 Celtic F.C. season''' began with a significant change. Former club captain [[Billy McNeill]] took over from [[Jock Stein]].<ref name=":2">{{Cite book |last=Woods |first=Pat |title=Glory and the Dream: History of Glasgow Celtic Football Club, 1887-1986 |date= |publisher=Mainstream Publishing |year=1986 |isbn=978-1851580545 |edition=1st}}</ref> Stein stepped down after 13 incredibly successful years, during which he led the club to numerous triumphs. His final season, however, was disappointing, with Celtic finishing fifth in the league, missing out on European football qualification, and failing to secure any silverware in the domestic cups.

[[Billy McNeill]] returned to Celtic after a promising spell managing [[Aberdeen F.C.]], where he finished as runners-up in both the Premier Division and the Scottish Cup.<ref name=":0">{{Cite news |last=Paul |first=Ian |date=30 May 1978 |title=McNeill takes Clark with him to Parkhead |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=Ufw9AAAAIBAJ&sjid=P0kMAAAAIBAJ&hl=es&pg=3128%2C6939134 |work=[[The Glasgow Herald]]}}</ref> During the summer, McNeill appointed his former defensive partner and assistant at Aberdeen, [[John Clark (footballer, born 1941)|John Clark]], as his assistant at Celtic[[Aberdeen F.C.|.]]<ref name=":0" /> and brought in [[Frank Connor]] to manage the Reserve team, [[Jimmy Lumsden]] also joined as a player with additional coaching responsibilities for the reserve team. In the transfer market McNeill made two significant signings in the autumn: winger [[David Provan (footballer, born 1956)|David Provan]] from [[Kilmarnock F.C.]]<ref name=":1">{{Cite news |last=Paul |first=Ian |date=19 September 1978 |title=£120,000 Provan goes to Celtic |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=RgI-AAAAIBAJ&sjid=cUkMAAAAIBAJ&hl=es&pg=5354%2C4248865 |work=[[The Glasgow Herald]]}}</ref> and midfielder [[Murdo MacLeod]] from [[Dumbarton F.C.]]. Both were young and talented players who would go on to play key roles at Celtic for many years. Additionally, former Celtic players [[Bobby Lennox]] and [[Vic Davidson]] returned to the club later in the season.

[[Celtic F.C.|Celtic]] made a bright start in the league during August and early September. However, their form became inconsistent from October through December, and they dropped several points. Harsh winter weather caused all league games in January and February to be postponed. When the league resumed in March, Celtic's form improved significantly, helped by the return of Captain [[Danny McGrain]], who had been out with a serious long term injury. The club faced a busy schedule in May, playing six games, with the final match against [[Rangers F.C.]] becoming the decisive fixture for the league title. A victory in this match would ensure the league flag returned to Celtic Park in McNeill's first season as manager. In a dramatic encounter, Celtic triumphed 4-2, despite being down to 10 men for most of the game—a match that remains fondly remembered by Celtic supporters.<ref>{{Cite web |title=1979-05-21: Celtic 4-2 Rangers, Premier Division |url=https://www.thecelticwiki.com/1979-05-21-celtic-4-2-rangers-premier-division/ |website=Celtic Wiki}}</ref>

In the League Cup, Celtic progressed to the semi-finals but were narrowly defeated by Rangers 3-2 after extra time.

In the Scottish Cup, a fifth-round replay loss to Aberdeen allowed Celtic to focus solely on their pursuit of the Premier Division title.

Billy McNeill's first season was regarded as a success, restoring the club to the top of Scottish football and allowing the club to make a return to compete in Europe's premier competition the following season.<ref name=":2" />

== Pre-season and friendlies ==

Due to the [[1978 FIFA World Cup]], Celtic only managed 2 pre-season friendlies plus the [[Jock Stein]] Testimonial game against Liverpool two days after the League opener against [[Greenock Morton F.C.|Morton]].

{{football box collapsible|id=Montrose v Celtic|date=29 July 1978|time=|round=[[Exhibition game#Club football|Friendly]]|score=1-3|report=https://www.thecelticwiki.com/1978-07-29-montrose-1-3-celtic-friendly/|team1=[[Montrose F.C.|Montrose]]|goals1=[[Bobby Livingstone|Livingstone]] {{goal|20}}|team2=Celtic|goals2=[[Roy Aitken|Aitken]] {{goal|23}}, [[George McCluskey|McCluskey]] {{goal|56}}, {{goal|74}}|stadium=[[Links Park]]|location=[[Montrose, Angus|Montrose]], Scotland|attendance=2,300|referee=R.B Valentine (Dundee)|result=W}}{{football box collapsible|id=Arsenal v Celtic|date=8 August 1978|time=|round=[[Exhibition game#Club football|Friendly]]|score=0-3|report=https://www.thecelticwiki.com/1978-08-08-celtic-0-3-arsenal-friendly/|team1=Celtic|goals1=|team2=[[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]]|goals2=[[Andy Lynch (footballer)|Lynch]] {{goal|29}}, [[Roddie MacDonald|MacDonald]] {{goal|76}}, [[Frank Stapleton|Stapleton]] {{goal|83}}|stadium=[[Celtic Park]]|location=[[Glasgow]],Scotland|attendance=24,000|referee=K. Hope (Glasgow)|result=L}}{{football box collapsible|id=Celtic v Liverpool|date=14 August 1978|time=19:30 GMT|round=[[Exhibition game#Club football|Jock Stein Testimonial]]|score=2–3|report=https://www.thecelticwiki.com/1978-08-14-celtic-2-3-liverpool-jock-stein-testimonial/|team1=Celtic|goals1=[[Ronnie Glavin|Glavin]] {{goal|21}}, [[Tom McAdam|McAdam]] {{goal|44}}|team2=[[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]]|goals2=[[Alan Kennedy|Kennedy]] {{goal|32}} [[Kenny Dalglish|Dalglish]] {{goal|40}}, {{goal|55}}|stadium=[[Celtic Park]]|location=[[Glasgow]], Scotland|attendance=62,000|referee=Brian McGinlay (Balfron)|result=L}}

